// Translate in place: replace the //RS lines below with the 1:1 Jet port (keep `// Rust: <file>:<line>` anchors).
//RS //! Regression battery for four codegen ICEs (I2/I3 violations) where the
//RS //! front end emitted Rust that rustc rejected instead of compiling correct
//RS //! code. Each case must pass the front end and — when rustc is available —
//RS //! build (rustc accepting the output is the whole point: a rejection here is
//RS //! a P0 front-end soundness bug, invariant I2).
//RS //!
//RS //! B1 — `JSON.Text(x)` on a borrowed (`view`) parameter moved out of the
//RS //!      borrow; sema must insert a clone.
//RS //! B2 — field access on a core struct (`ProcessResult.code`) mangled the field
//RS //!      name to `__jet_code`; the core struct has unprefixed fields.
//RS //! B3 — `.get(k)` on a `Map` bound from an `Object(root)` pattern lowered to
//RS //!      list indexing; the binding must keep its `Map` type.
//RS //! B4 — `for k, v in recv.field { … }` parsed `recv.field { … }` as a struct
//RS //!      literal instead of a loop body.
//RS //! B5 — `buf[i] = x` on a fixed-size `[T#N]` array left `IndexKind` at its
//RS //!      `Unknown` default (sema's write-side match only knew `List`/`Map`/
//RS //!      `User`, unlike the read-side `infer_index`); the TIR subset gate
//RS //!      excludes any `Unknown`-kind index assign, so codegen (TIR is the
//RS //!      only path, R7) panicked with an I2 ICE instead of emitting code.
//RS 
